diff --git a/LOGBOOK.md b/LOGBOOK.md
index 3e5e0245b4df57a3daac3250d0df45013cdb25c4..b0d7f6151356821031d6d931ca512baeb743c8b0
--- a/LOGBOOK.md
+++ b/LOGBOOK.md
@@ -5,6 +5,12 @@
 
 ## 2026-08-11
 
+### 2112 — LibSSH caller-cancellation test owns a deterministic remote lifecycle (BUG-260811-3qdo3e)
+- ROOT CAUSE: The real-sshd cancellation test opened two `sleep 2` exec channels while injecting a `ManualFixtureClock`. Caller cancellation correctly detached the pending read, but the following channel disposal still had to advance libssh2 cleanup behind the bridge-owned POSIX service and remote process exit. Because the manual clock also owned the channel-close deadline and was no longer advanced, cleanup could not expire independently; suite completion depended on two wall-clock subprocess lifetimes and could hang when socket/sshd retirement lost the favorable scheduling race. A clean baseline measured 4.116 seconds for this test, matching the two sequential sleeps.
+- IMPLEMENTATION: One EOF-driven `cat` channel now proves both properties without a sleep or timeout workaround. Advancing the manual clock leaves the idle read pending; cancelling it returns the exact channel-scoped, same-operation-retry, no-teardown error. The same channel then echoes a payload after `finishWriting`, a sibling exec succeeds, the connection remains ready, and transport close reports `.zero` owned resources.
+- VERIFICATION: The exact test passes 30/30 focused invocations and fell to 0.193–0.275 seconds in aggregate runs. The serialized 24-test real-sshd suite, recursive Swift format lint, `swift build`, `make check-libssh2`, and `make validate-libssh2` exit 0. Twenty consecutive unfiltered `swift test --skip-build` runs each pass all 426 tests with exit 0 and no new sshd listener. The pre-existing `TASK-260715-1u2vpc` approved-algorithm matrix remains intact.
+- ANOMALY: Adding `Process.waitUntilExit()` after the fixture's existing `isRunning` polling produced a reproducible Foundation wait deadlock in an unrelated socket-failure case; a process sample captured the stack and the attempted change was fully reverted. The original bounded sshd stop logic remains unchanged.
+
 ### 2044 — UDP teardown snapshots gain an awaitable completion seam (BUG-260728-2j25tu)
 - ROOT CAUSE: Peer EOF was published by `HEVUDPAssociationConnection.close` before `connectionClosed` removed the owner from the adapter and released queued-byte accounting. A peer-observable EOF therefore could not prove that adapter-internal snapshots had settled.
 - IMPLEMENTATION: Every connection close now participates in a lock-protected pending-teardown barrier. The barrier completes only after adapter bookkeeping and, for locally initiated closes, registry cleanup. All post-EOF adapter snapshot assertions in `HEVUDPDatagramAdapterTests` await this seam; the suite is serialized to keep its blocking socket fixtures from starving one another under full-suite load. No adapter cancellation or generation semantics changed, and no sleep, retry, eventually assertion, or wall-clock polling was added.
